**1. Use stable keys**
```jsx
// Bad: index keys cause remounts and state bugs when items are reordered or inserted
{items.map((item, i) => <Row key={i} item={item} />)}
// Good
{items.map(item => <Row key={item.id} item={item} />)}
```

**2. Memoize the row and keep its props stable**
`React.memo` only helps if the props stay the same between renders. Inline functions and objects break it.
```jsx
const Row = React.memo(function Row({ item, onSelect }) {
  return <li onClick={() => onSelect(item.id)}>{item.name}</li>;
});

function List({ items }) {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);
  const onSelect = useCallback(id => setSelected(id), []); // stable reference
  return <ul>{items.map(item => <Row key={item.id} item={item} onSelect={onSelect} />)}</ul>;
}
```
Avoid passing things like `style={{...}}`, `onClick={() => ...}` or `data={items.filter(...)}` directly to memoized rows.

**3. Keep state local**
If typing in a search box re-renders 1,000 rows, move that input's state into a small component of its own. You can also pass `isSelected={item.id === selected}` (a boolean) instead of `selected`, so only the two rows whose selection changed re-render.

**4. Memoize derived data**
```jsx
const visible = useMemo(() => items.filter(matches).sort(byName), [items, query]);
```

**5. Virtualize long lists (hundreds of rows or more)**
Only render the rows that are on screen, using `@tanstack/react-virtual` or `react-window`. For big lists this usually beats every other fix combined.

**6. Defer expensive updates**
`useDeferredValue(query)` or `startTransition` keeps typing responsive while the list catches up.

**7. Measure before and after**
Use the React DevTools Profiler with "Highlight updates when components render" turned on to see which rows re-render and why. If you're on React 19 with the React Compiler enabled, steps 2 and 4 mostly happen automatically.

