`textureSample` picks a mip level using screen-space derivatives. The GPU shades pixels in 2×2 groups (quads) and gets the derivatives by comparing the texture coordinates across the four pixels. If your `if` depends on a per-pixel value (a vertex input, a texture read, anything that can differ between neighbouring pixels), some pixels in a quad may skip the branch. Their coordinates are then undefined, and so is the mip level.

WGSL won't compile that. Its uniformity analysis traces where your condition's value comes from. If it can vary per pixel, the compiler reports a `derivative_uniformity` error. `dpdx`, `dpdy`, `fwidth`, `textureSampleBias` and `textureSampleCompare` have the same rule.

```wgsl
// error: derivative_uniformity
if (in.mask > 0.5) {
  color = textureSample(tex, samp, in.uv);
}
```

A condition that is the same for every pixel is fine, such as a value from a `var<uniform>` buffer or a constant. The error only appears when the condition can vary per pixel.

## Fixes, best first

**1. Sample first, then choose.** This is usually the right fix, and the cost is normally small.
```wgsl
let sampled = textureSample(tex, samp, in.uv);
if (in.mask > 0.5) {
  color = sampled;
}
```

**2. Take the derivatives outside the branch and use `textureSampleGrad` inside it.** Use this when the sample is expensive or there are many of them. Mipmapping still works correctly.
```wgsl
let ddx = dpdx(in.uv);
let ddy = dpdy(in.uv);
if (in.mask > 0.5) {
  color = textureSampleGrad(tex, samp, in.uv, ddx, ddy);
}
```

**3. Use `textureSampleLevel(tex, samp, in.uv, 0.0)`.** This is allowed anywhere because you give the level yourself, so no derivatives are needed. You lose mipmapping, so a minified texture will shimmer. It suits textures without mips, lookup tables, and full-screen passes.

**4. Turn the check off.** Only do this when you know the branch is uniform and the analysis can't prove it:
```wgsl
diagnostic(off, derivative_uniformity);   // whole module
// or just for one statement:
@diagnostic(off, derivative_uniformity) if (in.mask > 0.5) { ... }
```
If the branch really does vary per pixel, this leaves you with undefined results, which tend to show up as seams at the branch edges.

The barrier functions are different. `workgroupBarrier`, `storageBarrier` and `workgroupUniformLoad` in non-uniform control flow are always errors and can't be turned off, so they have to be moved out of the branch.

If you paste the shader, I can tell you which value makes the analysis treat your condition as varying per pixel.

There's no game source in this directory, just a WebGPU skill, and that skill doesn't cover native Vulkan. So this is a general setup for your engine. You're on macOS, so it assumes the LunarG Vulkan SDK running over MoltenVK.

## 1. Install the SDK

Install the **Vulkan SDK for macOS** from LunarG. It includes `VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation`, MoltenVK and `vkconfig`. Then source its environment:

```sh
source ~/VulkanSDK/<version>/setup-env.sh   # sets VULKAN_SDK, VK_ICD_FILENAMES, VK_LAYER_PATH, DYLD paths
vulkaninfo --summary                          # sanity check: should list MoltenVK device
```

## 2. Enable the layer in code (debug builds only)

```cpp
#ifndef NDEBUG
constexpr bool kValidation = true;
#else
constexpr bool kValidation = false;
#endif

const char* kLayers[] = { "VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation" };

std::vector<const char*> exts = { /* surface exts from GLFW/SDL */ };
if (kValidation) exts.push_back(VK_EXT_DEBUG_UTILS_EXTENSION_NAME);
#ifdef __APPLE__
exts.push_back(VK_KHR_PORTABILITY_ENUMERATION_EXTENSION_NAME);   // required for MoltenVK
#endif

VkDebugUtilsMessengerCreateInfoEXT dbg{ VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_DEBUG_UTILS_MESSENGER_CREATE_INFO_EXT };
dbg.messageSeverity = VK_DEBUG_UTILS_MESSAGE_SEVERITY_WARNING_BIT_EXT |
                      VK_DEBUG_UTILS_MESSAGE_SEVERITY_ERROR_BIT_EXT;
dbg.messageType     = VK_DEBUG_UTILS_MESSAGE_TYPE_GENERAL_BIT_EXT |
                      VK_DEBUG_UTILS_MESSAGE_TYPE_VALIDATION_BIT_EXT |
                      VK_DEBUG_UTILS_MESSAGE_TYPE_PERFORMANCE_BIT_EXT;
dbg.pfnUserCallback = [](VkDebugUtilsMessageSeverityFlagBitsEXT sev,
                         VkDebugUtilsMessageTypeFlagsEXT,
                         const VkDebugUtilsMessengerCallbackDataEXT* d, void*) -> VkBool32 {
    fprintf(stderr, "[Vulkan] %s\n", d->pMessage);
    if (sev & VK_DEBUG_UTILS_MESSAGE_SEVERITY_ERROR_BIT_EXT) { /* __builtin_debugtrap(); */ }
    return VK_FALSE;                     // never abort the call
};

VkInstanceCreateInfo ci{ VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_INSTANCE_CREATE_INFO };
#ifdef __APPLE__
ci.flags |= VK_INSTANCE_CREATE_ENUMERATE_PORTABILITY_BIT_KHR;
#endif
ci.enabledLayerCount       = kValidation ? 1 : 0;
ci.ppEnabledLayerNames     = kLayers;
ci.enabledExtensionCount   = (uint32_t)exts.size();
ci.ppEnabledExtensionNames = exts.data();
ci.pNext = kValidation ? &dbg : nullptr;   // also catches errors in vkCreate/DestroyInstance
vkCreateInstance(&ci, nullptr, &instance);
// then create the persistent messenger via vkGetInstanceProcAddr("vkCreateDebugUtilsMessengerEXT")
```

On the device side, enable `VK_KHR_portability_subset` if the device reports it (MoltenVK always does).

Before enabling the layer, check that it's present with `vkEnumerateInstanceLayerProperties`. If it's missing, log a warning and continue without it, so the game still runs on machines without the SDK.

## 3. Optional checks: synchronization, GPU-assisted, best practices

You can turn these on in code with `VK_EXT_layer_settings`, chained into `VkInstanceCreateInfo::pNext`:

```cpp
VkBool32 on = VK_TRUE;
VkLayerSettingEXT settings[] = {
    { "VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation", "validate_sync",           VK_LAYER_SETTING_TYPE_BOOL32_EXT, 1, &on },
    { "VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation", "validate_best_practices", VK_LAYER_SETTING_TYPE_BOOL32_EXT, 1, &on },
    // { ..., "gpuav_enable", ... }  // GPU-assisted: slow, use for targeted runs
};
VkLayerSettingsCreateInfoEXT ls{ VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_LAYER_SETTINGS_CREATE_INFO_EXT };
ls.settingCount = std::size(settings);
ls.pSettings    = settings;
dbg.pNext = &ls;
```

You can also set them without recompiling, using environment variables:

```sh
export VK_INSTANCE_LAYERS=VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation     # force-enable on any build
export VK_VALIDATION_VALIDATE_SYNC=true
export VK_VALIDATION_VALIDATE_BEST_PRACTICES=true
export VK_VALIDATION_DUPLICATE_MESSAGE_LIMIT=5
export VK_LAYER_DEBUG_ACTION=VK_DBG_LAYER_ACTION_LOG_MSG
```

You can also use `vkconfig`, the SDK's GUI, which writes a layer override that applies to every Vulkan app you launch.

## 4. Practical tips

- **Name your objects** with `vkSetDebugUtilsObjectNameEXT`, so messages say "GBuffer albedo" instead of a raw handle.
- **Label command buffers** with `vkCmdBeginDebugUtilsLabelEXT`, so errors show which pass they came from.
- **Turn on synchronization validation regularly.** It finds the barrier bugs that only show up on other GPUs.
- **Leave the layer out of release builds.** It's a big CPU cost.
- **Use RenderDoc on Windows/Linux to test your real targets.** On Mac, RenderDoc doesn't support Vulkan, so use Xcode's Metal frame capture through MoltenVK there.
- **Silence a known-noisy message** by its VUID using `message_id_filter` (env var `VK_VALIDATION_MESSAGE_ID_FILTER`).

